\tr{\td{ENTER calls (slots copied)}\td{22.4 M (47.0 M)}\td{7.2 M (135.3 M)}\td{9.7 M (19.1 M)}}
}

\p{Readings. (a) A frame is 2.1 times (Init) to 7 times (Cedar slice) longer than what it keeps: everything that iterates over the LENGTH is wasted work - that was the hash and the comparison of PRUNE (5.2). (b) On the Cedar slice the far frames are the elephant: 16\% of the calls, 15\% of the new frames, but 46\% of the frame words (66 words each) and, before D19, 102 M re-hashed outer bindings. (c) "PRUNE returns the frame that was just entered" (same base, same length, the idea of the wave-5 report) is 0.6\% of the calls on Init and 1.7\% on the Cedar slice: not worth a test, not built. (d) A direct-mapped cache keyed by (top of the environment, mask) in front of PRUNE, emptied at every PUSH / ENTER (the only invalidation \code{\verb~|Runs~} can know without help), would answer 23.5\% of the calls on Init, 17\% on the Cedar slice, 10\% on reachability; not built, because of what 5.3 found.}
}

\subtree{
\title{5.2 Stage A: kept-slot PRUNE, sum hashes, REST cache (published 14:22:54Z; kept)}

\ul{
\li{PRUNE: the hash is \code{\verb~|Hash.fin~} of the sum of one independent term per kept binding (D17), the probe compares the kept positions and the header count (D16), the miss writes HOLE over the length and then the kept slots. The loop runs over the set bits of the mask (\code{\verb~|b &= b - 1~}), never over the length.}
\li{far PRUNE: the REST chain comes from the verified cache (D19); the near part is hashed like any frame.}
\li{NEU: the same sum hash over the arguments (D17); the two \code{\verb~|arraycopy~} calls of a new spine are a loop for at most 8 words (and none for an empty prefix); ENTER of at most 8 slots is a loop.}
\li{CHEAD index: two independent multiplications and the top bits instead of \code{\verb~|Hash.cell~} (two in sequence + \code{\verb~|fin~}).}
\li{OPEN bit table: the reset range is \code{\verb~|[word of the session's first record, word of the last record made]~} (records are appended), so \code{\verb~|mark~} no longer keeps a minimum and a maximum.}
}

\p{Identity: the counters of 5.1 are IDENTICAL before and after (frames found / new, records, run words, ticks, on Init and the Cedar slice), probe steps beyond the home slot too (frames 6,638,165 -> 6,643,160 on Init, 5,604,412 -> 5,608,534 on the Cedar slice; NEU 4,390,620 -> 4,395,034 and 1,446,145 -> 1,452,037: the sum hash spreads as the chained one did). Tests: \code{\verb~|M1RunsTest.keptSlotProbeKeepsIdentityExactly~} (6,000 random environments and masks against a model keyed by the dense content: HOLE at kept positions, saturated counts, far frames, FRAME over the dense slots, ENTER round trip, neighbours that must differ), \code{\verb~|restCacheIsVerifiedByContent~}, \code{\verb~|hashesSpreadConsecutiveIdsAndShiftedPositions~}.}

\p{Measured, base (the main tree of 13:20Z) against stage A, both built from the same foreign files:}

\table{
\tr{\th{corpus}\th{instructions G}\th{CPU s / wall s, median of two interleaved runs (locked batch A2, 16:05-16:07Z, load average 58-79)}}
\tr{\td{Init}\td{139.0 -> 131.2 (-5.6\%)}\td{7.99 / 8.08 -> 7.36 / 7.46 (-7.9\% CPU)}}
\tr{\td{Std}\td{223.2 -> 209.9 (-6.0\%)}\td{13.40 / 13.51 -> 12.30 / 12.42 (-8.2\%)}}
\tr{\td{Cedar slice}\td{47.0 -> 38.8 (-17.4\%)}\td{3.38 / 3.67 -> 2.69 / 2.71 (-20.4\%)}}
\tr{\td{reachability}\td{59.4 -> 56.8 (-4.4\%)}\td{8.19 / 8.31 -> 8.12 / 8.24 (-0.9\%; the two pairs: 7.98 -> 7.88, 8.39 -> 8.36)}}
}

\p{(A first locked batch at 14:28-14:34Z gave Init 8.18 -> 7.69 s CPU, Std 13.96 -> 12.52, Cedar slice 3.50 -> 2.76, reachability 8.68 -> 8.38, but it fell into the window in which the host was thrashing; the orchestrator declared the timings of that window void.) Profile after stage A (\code{\verb~|work/m1/perf/*-a2.txt~}): \code{\verb~|Runs.pruneNear~} 11.6\% -> 9.0\% of the process on Init, 19.1\% -> 11.4\% on the Cedar slice (with \code{\verb~|sameMasked~} 3.7\% -> \code{\verb~|sameKept~} 1.0\%, \code{\verb~|restChain~} + \code{\verb~|same~} 5.1\% -> \code{\verb~|sameChain~} + \code{\verb~|restOf~} 3.0\%), 23.7\% -> 23.6\% on reachability.}
}

\subtree{
\title{5.3 What the probe costs now: the load of the table slot}

\p{\code{\verb~|perf annotate~} of the three probe functions on reachability (one or two giant declarations, tables of 64-256 MiB):}

\table{
\tr{\th{function (share of the process)}\th{the load of the table slot}\th{the frame / record load after it}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|Runs.pruneNear~} (23.6\%)}\td{79\% (\code{\verb~|rtab[slot]~})}\td{11\%}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|Eval.table~} = the inlined record probe (11.9\%)}\td{76\% (\code{\verb~|vtab[slot]~})}\td{14\%}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|Values.neu~} (9.7\%)}\td{83\% (\code{\verb~|vtab[slot]~})}\td{5\%}}
}

\p{About 39\% of reachability is waiting for intern-table slots that miss the cache; hashing, comparing and writing are noise there. On Init (tables of 64 + 128 KiB against a 64 KiB first-level cache) the same load is 37\% of \code{\verb~|pruneNear~}, and the whole process has 0.8\% mispredicted branches (229.6 M) and 0.84\% first-level misses (312 M), about a tenth of the time each. Experiments that follow from it:}

\table{
\tr{\th{experiment}\th{result}\th{verdict}}
\tr{\td{smaller base tables (\code{\verb~|-Drlvm.machine.sizes~}, records / vtab / run words / rtab / pairs / plog), Init CPU s, two runs each}\td{14,15,16,14,12,10 (default): 7.55 / 7.47; 12,13,14,12,10,8: 8.02 / 7.91; 11,..: 8.13 / 8.13; 10,..: 8.27 / 8.27; Cedar slice 2.62-2.72 in all four}\td{REJECTED (growth costs more than the misses; M6's sizes stand)}}
\tr{\td{front cache of recent frames (256 cells \code{\verb~|(hash, epoch, frame)~}, verified by content) in PRUNE}\td{answers 48\% of all frame lookups on Init (81\% of the found ones), 51\% on the Cedar slice, 68\% of the found ones on reachability - and costs: Init 7.45-7.50 -> 7.59 s, 132.1 -> 133.5 G instructions; reachability 8.36-8.47 -> 8.69}\td{REJECTED}}
\tr{\td{the same + front cache of closure / suspension records (1,024 pairs; in the simulation it answers 83\% of the records that are found on Init, 76\% on the Cedar slice, 88\% on reachability)}\td{Init 7.40-7.50 -> 7.63 s, 134.6 G; reachability 8.30-8.45 -> 8.60}\td{REJECTED}}
\tr{\td{PRUNE + record in one operation, the record's slot loaded early ALWAYS}\td{reachability 8.29 -> 8.20 s (8.36 / 8.23 / 8.29 against 8.35 / 8.10 / 8.20)}\td{not enough alone}}
\tr{\td{the same, but no early load for a frame whose hash was looked up a moment ago (D20)}\td{reachability 8.29 -> 7.69 s (7.81 / 7.66 / 7.69): -7.2\%; Init, Std, Cedar slice within +-0.5\%}\td{KEPT as an operation (5.4)}}
}

\p{Why the front caches lose: a lookup that a small recency cache can answer is a lookup whose table slot is still in the cache - the misses are the NEW keys (60\% of the frames, 96\% of the applications on reachability) and the old ones. A cache in front of a table only pays where it replaces work, not where it replaces a load that would have hit. The same argument retires the (environment, mask) cache of 5.1 (d). And why the early load must be predicted: when the frame exists, the speculative load fetches a cold line for nothing; the 256 recent hashes tell "exists" for 68-81\% of the found frames at the price of one load and one store.}
}

