}

\subtree{
\title{5.5 Storage strategies (owner note 8): what the distributions support}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Inline frames of 1 or 2 kept slots in the closure record} (no run, one probe): 44\% of the closure probes on Init and 42\% on the Cedar slice have such a frame. NOT BUILT, for two measured reasons. (1) The second probe is cheap exactly where sessions are small (after stage A a PRUNE of a small frame is a few kept-slot terms, one table word and one comparison), and where sessions are large the two loads overlap with D20; my ESTIMATE for inlining is about 1\% of Init (not measured: it cannot be tried without the other packages). (2) The frame word of a closure is read directly in M2, M4, M5, M6 and M7 (\code{\verb~|Runs.enter(m, vh[o + B])~}, \code{\verb~|Match~}, \code{\verb~|Code~} capture slots, \code{\verb~|Debug~}, \code{\verb~|Show~}): every one of them would have to go through an accessor that tests the strategy.}
\li{\strong{Inline arguments of a NEU with 1 or 2 arguments}: 35\% of the applications on Init. Not built: spines are read in place by M3, M4 and M7 (\code{\verb~|rs[run + RUN_H + j]~}), and word c of a NEU is its hash, which \code{\verb~|Hash.ofRecord~} reads.}
\li{\strong{Sparse frames \code{\verb~|(mask, kept values)~} for long environments}: this is the strategy the Cedar slice supports (length 19.4 against 4.6 kept for new frames; far frames 66 words each). Estimate from the histograms: frame words 90.3 M -> about 36 M (4 words of header and mask + the kept slots), i.e. 12,832 -> about 6,100 run words per declaration. The target of 4,000 cannot be reached by a representation: the kept slots (19.4 M) and the spines (12.2 M) alone are 3,920 words per declaration - below that only fewer frames help (M2: tabling). NOT BUILT in this wave: after stage A the time of PRUNE no longer depends on the length, so what is left to gain is the memory (page faults of grown slabs, 8 workers' cache footprint), against a layout change with direct readers in M6 (\code{\verb~|Debug~}, \code{\verb~|Show~}), M7 (\code{\verb~|Match~}, \code{\verb~|Code~}) and M4 (\code{\verb~|Conv.ccloFrom~} through \code{\verb~|Runs.len / arg~}). The cheapest first step would be far frames only (46\% of the Cedar slice's frame words; nobody but \code{\verb~|Runs~} reads their near part by index): header, hash, REST word, the mask as three immediates (so that the generic walkers of \code{\verb~|Debug~} / \code{\verb~|Show~} see harmless literals), the kept values.}
}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{4. Not done / open}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Far frames are still O(depth) per PRUNE and per ENTER, but cheaply}: since D19 a far PRUNE no longer hashes the outer bindings, it compares them with the cached chain (\code{\verb~|Arrays.equals~}, 44 slots on average on the Cedar slice; \code{\verb~|Runs.sameChain~} + \code{\verb~|restOf~} were 3.0\% of that corpus before the comparison was vectorised) and ENTER copies them. O(1) would need to know that the prefix of the slab was not rewritten; \code{\verb~|Eval.bind~} and \code{\verb~|MachineOps.in~} write \code{\verb~|es~} directly and every judgment truncates \code{\verb~|m.ep~}, so \code{\verb~|Runs~} cannot know without a rule for the other packages. Measured frequency (the item of ISA 2.4): 16\% of the PRUNEs of the Cedar slice, 0.03\% of Init's, none on reachability.}
\li{\strong{Sparse frames for long environments} (the Cedar slice's 12,832 run words per declaration): designed and estimated in 5.5, not built. The target "under 4,000" is out of reach of any representation (kept slots + spines are 3,920).}
\li{\strong{The young set} (5.3; requests.log 15:58Z to M6): the way to remove the table-slot misses of giant sessions rather than overlap them. A \code{\verb~|Machine~} design.}
\li{\strong{D20 in \code{\verb~|Infer~}}: five places of \code{\verb~|Infer~} (M5; lines 333, 933, 1226, 1612, 1971 at 15:43Z) still PRUNE and then probe; requested 15:43Z, M5's call. The LET suspension (\code{\verb~|mkSusp(.., true)~}) has no combined form.}
\li{\strong{D20 was measured with my fake of \code{\verb~|Eval~}, not with M2's published \code{\verb~|Eval~}} (same three calls; M2's own corpus runs were in progress at 16:49Z). With M2's switch \code{\verb~|m2a~} most tabling probes of reachability disappear (M2: records 20.6 -> 13.5 M), so the share that D20 can overlap is smaller there.}
\li{\strong{\code{\verb~|m.nats~} ownership} (D3): the guard detects a reissued id with the store's 32-bit content hash and the limb count; it is a detection, not a proof. (The machine has had its own overlay since M6's 09:45Z.)}
\li{\strong{The warm figures of owner note 12} (second pass in one process, 8 workers, pipelined): \code{\verb~|-X passes=N~} did not exist when this was measured; 5.2 and 5.4 give cold wall clock beside CPU time, one worker and (batch F) eight.}
\li{The JVM-only probe table of 3.1 predates stage A and was not repeated (the native profile of 5.3 replaces it).}
}
}
